concept structs {
  name     = "Structs & Methods"
  summary  = "Named field records with impl blocks; self is implicit and always by reference."
  related  = [enums, traits, reference_semantics]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "A `struct` is a named record of typed fields. Methods are declared in an `impl` block. `self` is implicit in type and **always by reference** — there is no `&` in wscript."
    code "rust" { source = <<'SRC'
struct Player { name: string, hp: int }

impl Player {
    fn new(name: string) -> Player {          // associated function:
        Player { name: name, hp: 100 }        // no self, called as
    }                                         // Player::new("wil")
    fn heal(self, amount: int) {
        self.hp += amount
    }
}

let p = Player::new("wil")
p.heal(20)                 // p.hp == 120
SRC
    }
    p "Associated functions (no `self`) live in inherent `impl` blocks and are called `Type::func(...)` — the constructor idiom."
    callout "Construction & mutation" { class = ["note"]  body = "Construct with `Type { field: value, … }` or an associated constructor. Fields are mutated in place (`p.hp = …`) — and because structs are reference types, that mutation is visible through every alias. See Reference Semantics." }
  }
}
